TI  - Determinants of fluoroscopy frequency and effect of standardized workflow optimization in robot-assisted pelvic channel screw placement: a single-center retrospective cohort study
AU  - Li J
AU  - Zhang W
AU  - Gong T
AU  - Li X
AU  - Yin K
AU  - Zhang X
AU  - Tai H
JO  - Journal of robotic surgery
PY  - 2026
VL  - 20
IS  - 1
SP  - 1180
DO  - 10.1007/s11701-026-04112-y
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Vaste cohorte rétrospective monocentrique (*Journal of Robotic Surgery*, nov. 2026, IF 2,2, 601 patients : 312 avant et 289 après optimisation) identifiant les 4 facteurs indépendants augmentant le recours à la scopie lors du vissage pelvien percutané (surface osseuse oblique au point d'entrée cortical IRR = 1,52, courbe d'apprentissage des 10 premiers cas IRR = 1,48, vissage du couloir S2 IRR = 1,26 et obésité IMC ≥ 30 IRR = 1,19) et démontrant qu'un protocole standardisé réduit de 27 % le nombre de clichés et le temps de scopie (16,2 s vs 21,8 s, p < 0,001) avec 98,7 % de précision intra-osseuse. | Points clés : Plus grande série analysant quantitativement les facteurs de difficulté technique du vissage pelvien percutané (601 patients, 98,7 % de précision). ; L'obliquité corticale au point d'entrée (IRR 1,52) et le corridor S2 (IRR 1,26) sont les deux défis anatomiques majeurs majorant le temps de scopie. ; Un protocole anti-dérapage au point d'entrée cortical réduit de 31,5 % le nombre de clichés sur les surfaces obliques et abaisse le temps de scopie moyen à 16,2 secondes. ; Enseignements techniques directement transposables au vissage pelvien percutané sous scopie/scanner pratiqué à Cochin. | Pratique Cochin : Directement applicable aux « Vissages percutanés du bassin (couloirs ilio-sacrés S1/S2 et colonnes acétabulaires) sous scopie Allura / guidage 3D » réalisés à l'Hôpital Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Robot-assisted percutaneous pelvic channel screw placement has been shown to significantly reduce intraoperative radiation exposure. However, even within the robot-assisted framework, fluoroscopy frequency still shows substantial within-group variation. Existing studies lack a systematic analysis of the determinants of fluoroscopy frequency and lack targeted workflow optimization strategies. To identify the independent determinants of fluoroscopy frequency in robot-assisted pelvic channel screw placement and to evaluate whether a standardized optimization protocol based on these factors can further reduce residual fluoroscopy exposure. We retrospectively enrolled patients with pelvic fractures who underwent robot-assisted percutaneous pelvic channel screw placement at Gansu Provincial Hospital between January 2020 and May 2025. Part A: The number of fluoroscopy sessions per case was the dependent variable. Multivariable negative binomial regression was used to analyze potential determinants, including surgeon experience stage, screw type, oblique bone surface at the entry point, BMI, and bone mineral density. Part B: Based on Part A results, we developed and implemented a standardized optimization protocol targeting modifiable factors, with December 2023 as the cutoff. We compared fluoroscopy frequency, fluoroscopy time, and screw accuracy between the pre-optimization group (n = 312) and the post-optimization group (n = 289). To address methodological time-trend confounding, we added interrupted time series analysis, propensity score matching, and a negative control subgroup analysis. This was a non-randomized, single-center, before-after design, and residual confounding cannot be completely excluded. Part A (n = 601): Multivariable negative binomial regression showed that oblique bone surface at the entry point (IRR = 1.52, 95% CI: 1.31-1.77, P 30 cases, IRR = 1.48, 95% CI: 1.25-1.75, P < 0.001), S2 screws (IRR = 1.26, 95% CI: 1.10-1.44, P = 0.001), and BMI ≥ 30 kg/m² (IRR = 1.19, 95% CI: 1.02-1.39, P = 0.026) were independent determinants of fluoroscopy frequency. Part B: The post-optimization group showed a significant reduction in fluoroscopy frequency compared with the pre-optimization group (9.8 ± 2.9 vs. 13.5 ± 3.7, P < 0.001). Fluoroscopy time also decreased (16.2 ± 5.4 s vs. 21.8 ± 6.9 s, P < 0.001). Screw accuracy showed no significant difference (98.7% vs. 98.1%, P = 0.52). The optimization effect was most pronounced in the oblique bone surface subgroup (31.5% reduction) and was 14.2% in the non-oblique subgroup. In the negative control subgroup (experienced surgeons, non-oblique, non-obese, S1 screws), fluoroscopy frequency did not change significantly before and after optimization (10.2 ± 2.6 vs. 10.5 ± 2.8, P = 0.41); however, post-hoc power for this subgroup was extremely low (approximately 0.10), so this null result should be regarded as inconclusive rather than as evidence of no effect. These findings suggest a possible risk-stratified specific effect of the optimization protocol, but time trends, the Hawthorne effect, and the unseparated multicomponent bundle cannot be excluded. In robot-assisted pelvic channel screw placement, oblique bone surface at the entry point, early surgeon experience stage, S2 screws, and obesity are independent determinants of fluoroscopy frequency. A standardized multicomponent optimization protocol targeting modifiable factors can safely reduce fluoroscopy frequency by approximately 27% without compromising screw accuracy. Optimization of fluoroscopy frequency should become a core indicator in robot-assisted surgical training and quality control. However, this was a non-randomized, single-center, before-after study; the optimization protocol was a multicomponent bundle whose individual contributions cannot be separated; and the oblique bone surface and negative control subgroups were underpowered. These findings require prospective validation.
