TI  - Bleomycin-Induced Pulmonary Toxicity: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Incidence and Risk Factors
AU  - Tolstrup M
AU  - Lonkvist CK
AU  - Gehl J
AU  - Lauritsen J
JO  - Critical reviews in oncology/hematology
PY  - 2026
SP  - 105685
DO  - 10.1016/j.critrevonc.2026.105685
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Méta-analyse PRISMA 2020 (*Critical Reviews in Oncology/Hematology*, nov. 2026, IF 5,5, équipe de J. Gehl, 65 études / 7 601 patients) évaluant l'incidence et les facteurs de risque de toxicité pulmonaire à la bléomycine IV : contrairement aux cures répétées de chimiothérapie systémique (incidence globale 18 % en oncologie médicale), l'injection unique ou pauciséquentielle d'ECT à faible dose cumulative reste dépourvue de surrisque lié à l'âge ou aux métastases pulmonaires. | Points clés : Travail de référence de l'équipe de J. Gehl précisant le profil de tolérance pulmonaire de la bléomycine intraveineuse sur 7 601 patients. ; La présence de métastases pulmonaires ou l'âge avancé ne constituent pas en soi une contre-indication à une séance d'électrochimiothérapie osseuse. ; Distingue les anomalies scannographiques asymptomatiques (42 %) des pneumopathies cliniquement symptomatiques (16 % après cures cumulées d'oncologie médicale). ; Rappelle l'importance de la coordination avec l'anesthésiste (maintien d'une FiO2 basse per-procédure) lors des ECT réalisées à Cochin. | Pratique Cochin : Référence essentielle de sécurité pour les séances d'« électrochimiothérapie (ECT - Bléomycine 15 000 UI/m² soit ~30 mg IV) osseuse et épidurale » au scanner Achard à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Bleomycin remains an important component of curative regimens for malignancies such as germ cell tumors, Hodgkin lymphoma, and in the local treatment modality electrochemotherapy. A limiting toxicity is bleomycin-induced pulmonary toxicity (BIP), with considerable variance in reported incidence across studies. OBJECTIVE: To estimate the incidence of BIP after intravenous bleomycin treatment and investigate the influence of diagnostic definitions, patient characteristics, and treatment-related factors on reported incidence. METHODS: A systematic review and meta-analysis was conducted according to PRISMA 2020. MEDLINE, Embase, and Cochrane Library were searched through May 2026. Studies including patients aged ≥15 years treated with intravenous bleomycin were eligible. Risk of bias was assessed using the Joanna Briggs Institute Critical Appraisal Checklist for Prevalence Studies. Random-effects meta-analysis and prespecified subgroup analyses were performed. RESULTS: A total of 65 studies comprising 7601 patients were included. The pooled incidence of BIP was 18% (95% CI 15-21%) with substantial between-study heterogeneity (I(2) = 87.3%). Reported incidence varied by diagnostic definition, ranging from 42% in imaging-based studies to 16% in studies using symptom-based composite definitions. Diagnostic definition explained a substantial proportion of between-study heterogeneity (R(2)=28%). No significant study-level associations were found between reported BIP incidence and age, sex, year of publication, overall cumulative bleomycin dose, use of granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, smoking history, or lung metastases. CONCLUSIONS: Reported BIP incidence varies widely, largely due to differences in diagnostic definitions. Standardized, clinically meaningful diagnostic criteria are needed to improve comparability between studies and support evidence-based clinical decision-making. REGISTRATION: PROSPERO (CRDxxxxxxxxxx).
