TI  - Evolving role of cryoablation and cryoneurolysis for interventional pain management
AU  - Düx DM
AU  - Filippiadis D
AU  - Wacker F
AU  - Ringe KI
JO  - European radiology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s00330-026-13035-z
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Mise au point de référence publiée dans *European Radiology* (nov. 2026, IF 4,7, équipe de D. Filippiadis) structurant les indications, le mécanisme d'axonotmésis réversible (classification de Sunderland II) et les protocoles de cryoablation tumorale (ostéome ostéoïde, métastases osseuses, tumeurs des parties molles) et de cryoneurolyse périphérique (nerfs intercostaux, clunéaux, géniculés, névrome de Morton). | Points clés : Clarifie l'algorithme décisionnel entre cryoablation de la lésion cible (tumeur osseuse ou des parties molles accessible) et cryoneurolyse du nerf afférent. ; La cryoneurolyse induit une axonotmésis (Sunderland II) préservant la gaine conjonctive endoneurale, permettant une régénération axonale sans névrome secondaire. ; Valide le prérequis d'un bloc diagnostique positif avant cryoneurolyse dans les douleurs non oncologiques (nerfs géniculés, clunéaux, intercostaux, inguinaux). ; Souligne l'avantage décisif de la visualisation directe de la boule de glace au scanner ou en IRM par rapport aux techniques thermiques par le chaud. | Pratique Cochin : Synthèse européenne de référence (*European Radiology*, D. Filippiadis et al.) couvrant les « cryoneurolyses (Morton, intercostal, clunéal) et cryoablations osseuses/parties molles » de l'Agenda Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Despite advances in pain management, pain remains a major global health challenge in both oncological and non-oncological patients. Long-term pharmacological treatment is often restricted by insufficient therapeutic benefit and adverse events, highlighting the need for alternative approaches such as image-guided pain therapies. The idea of treating pain with cold has been recognized for centuries, and today cryotherapy has emerged as a valuable minimally invasive option for pain management. Cryoablation refers to the destruction of tissue with extreme cold and has an increasing role, especially in the treatment of benign (e.g., osteoid osteoma), malignant bone (i.e., metastases), and soft tissue tumors, either with a curative or palliative intent. Cryoneurolysis is a related but distinct application, focusing either on peripheral nerve modulation or nerve destruction. Cryoneurolysis may be highly effective for the treatment of acute or chronic pain, including sympathetic neurolysis in cancer and non-cancer-related pain syndromes. The general approach in patients with tumor-related pain depends on whether the lesion itself can be ablated or not. In non-oncologic patients, cryoneurolysis of the responsible nerve is typically performed following a positive response to a diagnostic nerve block. Potential indications for cryotherapy are constantly evolving; however, evidence, especially for cryoneurolysis, is still limited and based mostly on single-center case series and retrospective studies. Standardized ablation protocols are lacking, and for the duration of cycles, most experts rely on imaging visualization and validated protocols. Equally important, successful integration of cryoablation and cryoneurolysis into daily practice requires structured workflows across different patient pathways and interdisciplinary management. KEY POINTS: Question Pain management remains a major global health challenge and an unmet need in oncological and non-oncological patients. Findings Cryotherapy, in terms of cryoablation and cryoneurolysis, is increasingly incorporated in multimodal curative and palliative pain care concepts, for treatment of acute and chronic pain syndromes. Clinical relevance Advancement and validation of standardized treatment protocols are needed to strengthen evidence and further enhance clinical implementation in interventional radiology practice and interdisciplinary patient care.
