AU  - Ungar L
JO  - Pain and therapy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s40122-026-00902-2
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte sur 224 adultes (*Pain and Therapy*, nov. 2026, IF 3,8) évaluant le parcours interventionnel de la névralgie pudendale : bloc pudendal diagnostique réalisé chez 59,4 % des patients et radiofréquence pulsée (PRF) chez 28,6 %, avec identification d'un bloc diagnostique positif (OR ajusté = 4,88) et d'une étiologie post-chirurgicale confirmée (OR = 2,83) comme facteurs prédictifs majeurs de réponse aux thérapies de neuromodulation. | Points clés : Près de 60 % des patients bénéficient d'un bloc pudendal et près de 30 % d'une radiofréquence pulsée, piliers du parcours mini-invasif. ; Un test anesthésique positif du nerf pudendal est le facteur prédictif indépendant le plus puissant (OR = 4,88) pour guider la neuromodulation. ; L'implantation chirurgicale lourde n'est finalement nécessaire que chez 8 % des patients grâce à l'efficacité des gestes percutanés. ; Conforte l'algorithme de prise en charge graduée (bloc test scanoguidé puis PRF / cryoneurolyse) pratiqué en Radiologie B à Cochin. | Pratique Cochin : Directement lié aux « infiltrations scanoguidées, radiofréquences pulsées et cryoneurolyses des nerfs pudendal et clunéal » programmées à l'Hôpital Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] INTRODUCTION: Pudendal neuralgia is an under-recognized cause of chronic, disabling neuropathic perineal pain affecting both women and men. Real-world data on its presentation, its treatment within a specialty pain service, and the factors that accompany escalation to interventional therapy are scarce. We hypothesized that an iatrogenic (postsurgical) origin and relief after a diagnostic pudendal nerve block would identify the patients who reach neuromodulation. METHODS: We performed a retrospective manual review of the complete electronic records of all adults with documented pudendal neuralgia, pudendal neuropathy, or pudendal nerve entrapment managed at a single tertiary pain clinic between 2009 and 2026. Demographics, clinical presentation, presumed etiology, treatments, and outcomes were abstracted from the free-text record. Ascertainment was in two stages: variables were first identified by structured text screening of the free-text record, and the two variables most susceptible to false-positive screening, a postsurgical etiology and receipt of neuromodulation, were then verified against the source charts by manual review of every screen-positive record. The chart-verified definitions are used throughout. Associations between binary variables were tested by the chi-squared test, with odds ratios (OR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI); factors associated with neuromodulation were assessed by multivariable logistic regression. RESULTS: Among 224 patients (138 women [61.6%], 86 men [38.4%]; median age 48.5 years, interquartile range 35-64), pain was worse when sitting in 57.6% and burning in 39.3%, and involvement was bilateral in 21.9%. Text screening identified a possible postsurgical onset in 46.4%, but chart re-adjudication confirmed a named operation preceding the pain in only 31 patients (13.8%), of whom 6 had undergone a clearly non-pelvic procedure; confirmed postsurgical onset was therefore less frequent than documented entrapment features (24.1%) and comparable to childbirth (15.9% of women). Care was heavily interventional: 59.4% received a pudendal nerve block, 28.6% pulsed radiofrequency, and 9.4% underwent surgical decompression. Neuromodulation was documented in the record of 64 patients (28.6%), but an electrode was placed in only 26 (11.6%) and a permanent device implanted in 18 (8.0%): spinal cord stimulation in 14, sacral neuromodulation in 2, dorsal root ganglion stimulation in 1, and pudendal nerve stimulation in 1. Three factors were independently associated with documented neuromodulation: relief after a diagnostic block (adjusted OR 4.88, 95% CI 1.91-12.46%), a refractory course (2.92, 1.52-5.60%), and chart-confirmed postsurgical onset (2.83, 1.17-6.82%); c-statistic 0.76. In an exploratory model restricted to the 18 implanted patients, a refractory course (7.40, 1.98-27.61%) and postsurgical onset (4.24, 1.29-13.94%) remained associated with the outcome whereas relief after a block did not (5.90, 0.72-48.31%). CONCLUSIONS: In routine pain-clinic practice, pudendal neuralgia is managed along an interventional pathway, but device implantation is considerably less common than the frequency with which neuromodulation is discussed. A confirmed postsurgical onset is less common than text screening suggests, yet is disproportionately associated with escalation. Being observational, these findings describe how care proceeds rather than establishing cause.
