AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Vaste cohorte oncologique (*Neurosurgery*, oct. 2026, IF 3,9) de 240 patients (241 interventions) traités par vissage pédiculaire percutané augmenté au ciment (cement-augmented PPSF) pour fracture vertébrale par compression pathologique d'origine métastatique, avec un suivi médian de 5,0 ans chez les survivants : taux de défaillance mécanique nécessitant une reprise chirurgicale exceptionnellement bas de 2,1 % (5/240), amélioration significative et durable de la douleur et du statut fonctionnel/neurologique. | Points clés : Sur 240 patients oncologiques avec un suivi médian de 5 ans chez les survivants, le taux de démontage mécanique nécessitant une reprise n'est que de 2,1 % (5/240). ; Démontre qu'une fixation percutanée cimentée sans greffe postéro-latérale ouverte reste mécaniquement fiable même chez les longs survivants sous thérapies ciblées/immunothérapie. ; Améliore significativement et durablement les scores de douleur, la force motrice et l'autonomie fonctionnelle. | Pratique Cochin : Référence majeure pour les « Ostéosynthèses rachidiennes percutanées (vissage pédiculaire cimenté) et cimentoplasties des fractures vertébrales métastatiques » prises en charge à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Background and objectives Pathological vertebral compression fractures (VCFs) are a common and debilitating complication of metastatic cancer. Percutaneous pedicle screw fixation (PPSF) with cement augmentation is increasingly used to stabilize these fractures, but long-term construct durability and associated clinical outcomes remain incompletely defined. We sought to characterize the long-term mechanical durability of cement-augmented PPSF for oncological VCFs, evaluate outcomes, and explore factors associated with revision surgery. Methods We conducted a retrospective review of patients with cancer who underwent cement-augmented PPSF at a tertiary cancer center between 2015 and 2020. The primary outcome was implant failure requiring return to the operating room for revision stabilization. Cumulative incidence of revision was estimated. Associations between clinical and radiographic variables and revision were evaluated using univariable competing-risks regression. Secondary outcomes included pain scores, functional status, strength, and neurological measures assessed pre- and post-operatively. Results A total of 240 patients with 241 surgeries were included (mean age 62.8 years). Median follow-up among surviving patients was 5.0 years. Five patients (2.1%) required revision surgery. Increasing age was associated with higher risk of revision in the univariable analysis (hazard ratio 1.12, 95% CI: 1.01-1.24, P = .046), while other investigated factors were not significantly associated with failure requiring revision. Significant improvements in pain and functional status were observed. Conclusion Cement-augmented PPSF demonstrated long-term durability for stabilization of oncological VCFs, with a low rate of revision surgery. Mechanical stability was accompanied by improvements in pain and functional status. Increasing age demonstrated an association with increased revision rates, although the low number of events limits definitive identification of predictors of failure.
