AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Enquête internationale multicentrique du réseau ISSVA (*Vascular Medicine*, oct. 2026, IF 2,2, 52 spécialistes de 16 pays dont 90 % en CHU et 92 % en RCP dédiée) détaillant les standards pratiques de sclérothérapie des malformations vasculaires à flux lent (veineuses et lymphatiques) : prédominance du trio éthanol (67 %), bléomycine (58 %) et polidocanol (52 %), l'éthanol étant unanimement classé comme l'agent le plus efficace mais le plus à risque de nécrose cutanée (94 %) et de lésion nerveuse (75 %), justifiant l'essor de la mousse de polidocanol, de la bléomycine et de l'éthanol gélifié en zones sensibles. | Points clés : Dans 92 % des centres experts internationaux (ISSVA), la prise en charge des malformations vasculaires à flux lent repose sur une décision de RCP multidisciplinaire. ; Hiérarchise clairement le profil de sécurité des 3 sclérosants majeurs : polidocanol et bléomycine permettent des traitements sous anesthésie locale avec un risque cutané et nerveux réduit par rapport à l'éthanol liquide. ; Rappelle l'importance du dépistage de la coagulopathie intravasculaire localisée (fibrinogène, D-dimères) avant sclérothérapie des malformations veineuses volumineuses. | Pratique Cochin : Référentiel international des pratiques pour les séances de « Sclérothérapie percutanée (Polidocanol / Bléomycine / Éthanol) et gestion péri-procédurale de la coagulation des malformations vasculaires » à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Background Slow-flow vascular malformations (SFVMs) are rare congenital anomalies that cause significant morbidity. Currently, no evidence-based therapy exists to improve SFVM symptoms and reduce its relapse in the long-term. Sclerotherapy is central to management and widely practiced, yet comprehensive international data are limited and no standardized guidelines exist. We aimed to evaluate current practice patterns, focusing on sclerotherapy and multidisciplinary care. Methods An international cross-sectional survey was distributed to experts in the International Society for the Study of Vascular Anomalies (ISSVA) network. The questionnaire addressed treatment indications, sclerosant use and availability, anesthesia, coagulation management, risk communication, and case-based decision-making. Responses were analyzed descriptively. Results Fifty-two physicians from 16 countries completed the survey. Respondents included interventional radiologists (69%), vascular medicine specialists (15%), and pediatric surgeons (8%) in academic centers (90%). Multidisciplinary consultation was reported by 92% of participants. Ethanol (67%), bleomycin (58%), and polidocanol (52%) were the most accessible, although ethanol was consistently rated as the most dangerous. Sclerosant choice varied by lesion type and scenario. The risks most frequently communicated were skin necrosis (92% for lymphatic malformations and 94% for venous malformations, respectively), pigmentary changes (70% and 75%), and nerve injury (68% and 75%), whereas systemic complications such as cardiovascular collapse (16% and 22%) or death (14% and 22%) were less often discussed. In children, general anesthesia was preferred (90% and 86%), whereas adults received local (49% and 41%) or general anesthesia (47% and 43%). Preprocedural coagulation testing and anticoagulation strategies showed marked heterogeneity, especially for venous malformations. Conclusions This survey demonstrates substantial variability in SFVM management, particularly regarding sclerosant selection, anesthesia, coagulation, periprocedural care, and risk communication, and identifies areas where future prospective and collaborative research will be needed before evidence-based recommendations can be developed.
