AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Revue critique publiée dans le *Journal of Bone Oncology* (oct./déc. 2026, IF 3,4) démontrant que la limite principale des modèles diagnostiques en oncologie osseuse réside dans la variabilité du standard histologique : la concordance de grade entre biopsie au trocart (CNB) et pièce opératoire dans les chondrosarcomes centraux chute de κ = 0,63 sur les os longs à κ = 0,12 dans le bassin. | Points clés : Dans les tumeurs cartilagineuses centrales du bassin, la concordance de grade entre biopsie au trocart et résection chute à κ = 0,12 (contre κ = 0,63 sur les os longs). ; Une biopsie osseuse rassurante (bas grade) sur une lésion pelvienne lytique ou agressivement rehaussée en IRM ne doit jamais faire récuser un haut grade sans relecture. ; Justifie la réalisation de carottes multiples dans des cadrans distincts lors des biopsies osseuses scanoguidées du bassin à Cochin. ; Plaide pour l'adoption systématique d'un gold standard composite associant imagerie multimodale, histologie et biologie moléculaire en RCP. | Pratique Cochin : Éclaire directement l'interprétation des « biopsies osseuses percutanées sous scanner (notamment du bassin et des os longs) » discutées en RCP Sarcome à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Multimodal imaging is central to the diagnosis, staging, and management of bone tumors. Radiography and computed tomography remain essential for initial assessment and cortical evaluation, magnetic resonance imaging provides superior soft tissue contrast and local staging accuracy, and positron emission tomography enhances detection of metastatic disease and treatment response. No single technique suffices across clinical scenarios. Advances in artificial intelligence and radiomics offer avenues to augment this framework, with demonstrated potential in lesion classification, segmentation, prognostication, and response prediction. This review advances a specific argument rather than surveying available technologies: that the principal constraint on artificial intelligence in musculoskeletal oncology is the stability of the reference standard rather than algorithmic capability. Ground truth in bone tumors is unstable on four grounds. Core needle biopsy samples a limited volume and may miss the highest-grade component: agreement between biopsy and resection grade in central chondrosarcoma falls from a weighted kappa of 0.63 in long bones to 0.12 in the pelvis. Expert interpretation is itself inconsistent, with interobserver kappa for grading cartilaginous tumors of 0.44 among musculoskeletal pathologists and 0.35 among radiologists. Molecular criteria have redefined entities within the past decade, so older datasets contain classes that no longer exist as defined. And histological classification does not reliably predict clinical behavior. Consequently, measurable accuracy has a ceiling set by the reference standard rather than the algorithm, and apparent failure on external validation may reflect label divergence between institutions rather than model deficiency. Evidence is graded throughout, and clinical readiness distinguished from technical performance. No system for primary bone tumor characterization has regulatory clearance in the United States or Europe, and formal appraisal of this literature confirms low methodological compliance and infrequent external validation. Progress will depend on multicenter validation and protocol harmonization, and, distinctively for this field, on composite reference standards, outcome-based endpoints, and explicit uncertainty modelling, since ground-truth instability can be mitigated but not eliminated.
