AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte monocentrique (*World Journal of Radiology*, oct. 2026, IF 2,5, Open-Access) portant sur 64 biopsies osseuses percutanées consécutives guidées par scanner : rendement diagnostique de 93,75 % (60/64) dès le premier prélèvement, concordance histologique de 100 % parmi les prélèvements contributifs, révision du diagnostic clinique pré-geste dans 9,4 % des cas (6/64) et 0 % de complication majeure SIR. | Points clés : Rendement diagnostique élevé de 93,75 % dès la première biopsie osseuse sous scanner, dominée par les localisations pelviennes complexes. ; Redressement du diagnostic clinique pré-procédural dans près de 1 cas sur 10 (9,4 %), justifiant l'histologie systématique avant ablation ou radiothérapie. ; Sécurité optimale : 0 % de complication majeure SIR et seulement 3,1 % d'événements mineurs locaux. ; Conforte les standards de biopsie osseuse coaxiale sous scanner pratiqués en Radio B à Cochin. | Pratique Cochin : Reflète directement l'activité quotidienne de « Biopsie osseuse ou disco-vertébrale sous scanner (crête iliaque, sacrum, membres) » de l'Agenda Interventionnel Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Background Computed tomography guided percutaneous bone biopsy provides tissue for the assessment of suspected primary and secondary bone lesions. Diagnostic yield, concordance with subsequent diagnostic assessment, and adverse events describe different aspects of performance. Their interpretation depends on case selection, outcome definitions, and the completeness and independence of diagnostic verification. Aim To describe diagnostic yield, recorded diagnostic concordance, and complications in a retrospective single-center cohort of image-guided bone biopsies. Methods We retrospectively reviewed 64 consecutive image-guided percutaneous bone biopsy procedures performed from January 2023 through December 2025. Diagnostic yield required clinically useful histopathology without repeat sampling. Recorded concordance used a composite of surgical histopathology, immunohistochemistry-supported assessment, or clinical and imaging follow-up. Non-diagnostic procedures remained in the full-cohort analysis. Complications were classified using the legacy Society of Interventional Radiology system. Proportions were summarized with exact binomial 95% confidence intervals (CI); no external hypothesis tests were performed. Results Diagnostic yield was 60/64 (93.75%; 95%CI: 84.8%-98.3%). Recorded concordance among 60 diagnostic, evaluable procedures was 60/60 (100%; 95%CI: 94.0%-100%). The full-cohort concordant diagnostic-result rate was 60/64 (93.75%) when four non-diagnostic procedures were counted as failures. Six biopsies revised the pre-procedural clinical diagnosis. Pelvic lesions were the most frequent sites. Two minor class B biopsy-tract fistulas occurred (2/64, 3.1%; 95%CI: 0.4%-10.8%); no major complications were recorded (0/64; 95%CI: 0%-5.6%). The heterogeneous cohort, incomplete verification reporting, and potentially non-independent composite reference standard limit interpretation of the conditional concordance estimate. Conclusion This cohort showed high diagnostic yield and two recorded minor complications. Conditional concordance does not establish unbiased diagnostic accuracy; independent verification and prospective confirmation are needed.
