AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Revue de synthèse (*Current Treatment Options in Oncology*, oct. 2026, IF 4,1) faisant le point sur l'apport des biomarqueurs radiomiques extraits de l'IRM conventionnelle dans les sarcomes des tissus mous : discrimination bas grade vs haut grade avant biopsie, prédiction précoce de la nécrose post-traitement néoadjuvant et stratification pronostique du risque métastatique. | Points clés : L'analyse quantitative de l'hétérogénéité IRM distingue avec une haute précision les sarcomes de haut grade (FNCLCC G2–G3) des tumeurs de bas grade. ; Permet d'orienter la biopsie percutanée vers le sous-volume tumoral (« habitat ») biologiquement le plus agressif. ; Surpasse les critères morphologiques RECIST 1.1 pour évaluer la nécrose tumorale réelle après traitement néoadjuvant ou ablation. ; Renforce la valeur ajoutée de l'expertise IRM ostéo-articulaire de Cochin au sein de la RCP Sarcome. | Pratique Cochin : Complète les bilans d'« IRM diagnostique, ciblage biopsique et suivi post-thérapeutique des sarcomes des parties molles » en centre de référence Sarcome à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Opinion statement Soft tissue sarcomas represent a diverse set of malignancies with equally diverse behavior, ranging from relatively indolent low-grade tumors to aggressive high-grade tumors with high metastatic potential. Initial diagnosis hinges on tissue biopsy for determining histologic subtype and grade, while conventional imaging plays critical roles in defining local tumor size and extent, staging, treatment response, and disease surveillance. However, conventional semantic interpretation of imaging studies overlooks much of the complex imaging data embedded in routine clinical studies. Radiomics encompasses the identification of novel disease biomarkers extracted from highly quantitative characterizations of tumor structure and heterogeneity and has shown promise in providing a deeper understanding of soft tissue sarcomas. Given the increasing usage of imaging in soft tissue sarcomas, particularly usage of MRI, it behooves both clinicians and radiologists to further the depth of understanding that may be gleaned from radiomics. These insights, although primarily experimental, represent a rapidly evolving landscape in soft tissue sarcoma diagnosis and management where radiomics will be increasingly integrated into clinical practice. The present work focuses on the latest updates with regards to the application of radiomics to the diagnosis, prediction of treatment response, assessment of treatment response, and prognosis of soft tissue sarcomas and how they may affect sarcoma management.
