AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte rétrospective (*Clinical Imaging*, oct. 2026, IF 1,8) issue de l'analyse de 256 989 comptes rendus d'IRM rachidiennes (6 709 mentionnant une lésion en hyposignal T1, 212 lésions solitaires suivies analysées selon le système Bone-RADS 2022 de la Society of Skeletal Radiology) : 87,7 % (186/212) des lésions solitaires étaient classables d'emblée en Bone-RADS 1 (« leave-alone » bénin certain) mais avaient pourtant généré 295 examens d'imagerie de suivi inutiles ; aucune lésion Bone-RADS 1 n'a évolué vers la malignité ni nécessité de biopsie sur le suivi. | Points clés : Sur 212 lésions rachidiennes solitaires en hyposignal T1 suivies en imagerie, 87,7 % (186/212) étaient en réalité des lésions bénignes Bone-RADS 1 ne nécessitant aucun contrôle. ; Aucune lésion classée Bone-RADS 1 n'a développé de critère d'agressivité ni requis de biopsie osseuse au cours du suivi. ; L'intégration du score Bone-RADS dans les comptes rendus d'IRM rachidienne à Cochin sécurise l'abstention et filtre efficacement les indications de biopsie vertébrale scanoguidée. | Pratique Cochin : Outil décisionnel direct pour le « Triage IRM des lésions vertébrales fortuites en hyposignal T1 (Bone-RADS 1 vs Bone-RADS 2/3/4) afin d'éviter les IRM de contrôle et biopsies rachidiennes inutiles » à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Purpose Incidental T1-hypointense bone lesions on spinal MRI frequently prompt follow-up. We retrospectively applied the 2022 Society of Skeletal Radiology Bone Reporting and Data System (Bone-RADS) framework to a historical cohort to assess concordance between observed lesion outcomes and Bone-RADS expectations and quantify imaging that would not have been recommended under the framework. Methods This retrospective cohort study was approved by the institutional Research Ethics Board with waiver of informed consent. Spine MRI reports from May 2013 to May 2023 were reviewed. Of 256,989 spine MRI examinations, 6709 reports contained the term "T1-hypointense," and 276 patients with recommended and completed lesion-directed follow-up met inclusion criteria. Bone-RADS was applied retrospectively to this follow-up-enriched cohort; no histopathological reference standard was available. Results Among 276 patients with incidental T1-hypointense bone lesions, 212 had solitary lesions eligible for retrospective Bone-RADS classification and 64 had multiple lesions, which were not classified. Of the 212, 186 were classified as Bone-RADS 1 and 26 as Bone-RADS 2/3. The 186 Bone-RADS 1 patients underwent 295 lesion-directed follow-up examinations that would not have been recommended under retrospective application of Bone-RADS. No lesion developed aggressive features or required biopsy or intervention. Median follow-up for Bone-RADS 1 lesions was 5.5 months (interquartile range, 3.0-21.5 months). Conclusion Observed outcomes were concordant with Bone-RADS expectations but do not constitute validation of the framework. Retrospective application identified follow-up examinations beyond Bone-RADS recommendations for lesions classified as Bone-RADS 1. Prospective multicenter evaluation against histopathological or long-term clinical reference standards is required.
