DO  - 10.1016/j.injury.2026.113714
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte sur 69 patients consécutifs (149 vis canulées de gros diamètre ≥ 7 mm : 123 vis trans-sacrées [TSS] et 26 vis ilio-sacrées [ISS]) traités en décubitus dorsal avec suivi moyen de 19,5 mois (*Injury*, sept. 2026, IF 2,2) : 100 % de trajet strictement intra-osseux au scanner post-opératoire, 0 % de complication neurovasculaire ou infectieuse, 0 % de descellement et 0 % de pseudarthrose (1 seul bris de vis S2 asymptomatique, soit 0,67 %). | Points clés : 100 % de précision intra-osseuse au scanner sur 149 vis pelviennes postérieures de gros diamètre (123 trans-sacrées et 26 ilio-sacrées) posées en décubitus dorsal. ; Le vissage trans-sacré (TSS) bi-cortical ilio-sacro-iliaque neutralise le conflit d'angulation avec la table en décubitus dorsal et offre une tenue mécanique supérieure. ; Zéro descellement, zéro pseudarthrose et 0 % de lésion radiculaire sacrée sur un suivi moyen de près de 20 mois. | Pratique Cochin : Directement applicable aux procédures de « Vissage percutané du bassin postérieur (vis ilio-sacrées et trans-sacrées S1/S2) » réalisées à l'Hôpital Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Introduction When patients are positioned supine for percutaneous screw insertion to achieve posterior pelvic ring fixation, the edge of the operating table can impede screw insertion trajectories, thereby increasing the risk of cortical perforation, especially during iliosacral screw (ISS) placement. Multiple screw insertion might also potentially increase the risk of cortical perforation. The aim of this study was to evaluate the feasibility, safety, and efficacy of inserting multiple (≥2) transsacral screw (TSS) and ISS in the supine position via the application of specific surgical strategies and techniques developed in our institution. Patients and methods Patients who underwent stabilization of posterior pelvic ring injuries via percutaneous insertion of multiple TSS and/or ISS in the supine position between May 2018 and December 2025 were included in this study. Distinct screw insertion combination patterns at different vertebral levels, which were primarily determined by the variable anterior-posterior width of the transverse osseous pathway at the first sacrum, were employed for posterior pelvic ring fixation. Wound infection and neurovascular complications associated with screws insertion procedure were recorded. Postoperative CT scans were performed to assess the position of the screws. At follow-up, radiographic evaluations were performed to evaluate the complications including screw loosening, screw breakage, and non-union. Results A total of 149 large-diameter (≥7 mm) screws, including 123 TSSs and 26 ISSs, were inserted in a total of 69 consecutive patients. No wound infection and neurovascular complication associated with the screw insertion procedure were found postoperatively. Postoperative CT imaging demonstrated that all inserted screws were intraosseous. All patients were followed for an average of 19.5 months (range, 6.6-38 months). Only one screw (S2 TSS) breakage was noted, and no screw loosening and no bone nonunion occurred. Conclusions Fluoroscopy-assisted percutaneous multiple large-diameter screws (TSS and ISS) insertion for posterior pelvic ring injuries with the patient positioned supine has been confirmed to be a feasible, safe, and effective surgical procedure.
