DO  - 10.1016/j.jvir.2026.109083
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte sur 92 séances de cryoablation chez 44 patients (*JVIR*, sept. 2026, taille médiane 6,9 cm, suivi médian 28,9 mois) rapportant 81,1 % de succès clinique, 78,4 % de réponse objective (24,3 % RC, 54,1 % RP), une concordance quasi parfaite entre volumétrie 3D et mRECIST (κ = 0,895), et seulement 5,3 % de complications majeures. | Points clés : Taux de succès clinique de 81,1 % et contrôle de la maladie (RC + RP + stabilité) de 94,6 % sur des desmoïdes volumineuses (médiane 6,9 cm). ; Concordance quasi parfaite (κ = 0,895) entre les critères mRECIST et la volumétrie 3D sur l'IRM de suivi post-cryoablation. ; Possibilité de retraitement itératif par cryoablation (26,3 % des patients) en cas de reliquat ou récidive focale, évitant les chimiothérapies au long cours. ; Profil de sécurité très favorable avec seulement 5,3 % d'événements indésirables de grade SIR ≥ 3. | Pratique Cochin : Au cœur de l'activité de « cryothérapie percutanée de tumeur desmoïde (creux sus-claviculaire, flanc, paroi, membres) » du Service de Radiologie B à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Purpose To determine whether image-guided cryoablation achieves durable clinical and radiologic response with an acceptable safety profile in patients with extra-abdominal desmoid tumors and whether volumetric response assessment concords with modified Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors-based classification MATERIALS AND METHODS: Patients treated between 2020 and 2024 were identified through electronic record. Those who underwent cryoablation were included for analysis. The primary outcome was clinical success, defined as a composite of no disease progression at last follow-up, no additional treatment, and symptom improvement. The secondary outcomes included radiologic response, symptom improvement, progression-free survival, and safety. Among 208 patients diagnosed with desmoid tumors, 44 (20.7%) underwent cryoablation, of whom 38 had follow-up data available; 92 cryoablation sessions were performed. Median tumor size was 6.9 cm (interquartile range, 5.5-9.2 cm), and median follow-up duration was 28.9 months (interquartile range, 14.3-37.3 months). Results The clinical success rate was 81.1%. Radiologic response at last follow-up was complete in 24.3%, partial in 54.1%, stable in 16.2%, and progressive in 5.4% of patients. Volumetric and modified Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors-based response classification showed almost-perfect agreement (κ = 0.895). Repeat cryoablation was performed in 26.3% of patients due to radiologic progression. Additional treatment modalities were required in 13.2% of cases. The 1- and 2-year progression-free survival rates were 76.5% (95% confidence interval, 58.2%-87.6%) and 67.4% (95% confidence interval, 46.5%-81.7%), respectively. Major adverse events (Society of Interventional Radiology grade, ≥Grade 3) occurred in 5.3% of patients. Conclusions Cryoablation is a safe and effective treatment option for selected patients with extra-abdominal desmoid tumors, achieving clinical success in the majority of patients. These findings support its role in the multidisciplinary management of desmoid tumors.
