AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude prospective sur 70 sarcomes des tissus mous en IRM 3T (*Quantitative Imaging in Medicine and Surgery*, nov. 2026) avec co-alignement spatial coupe-à-coupe IRM/anatomopathologie, démontrant que les paramètres Ktrans (DCE-MRI) et D* (IVIM) corrèlent fortement avec l'expression de VEGF et la densité microvasculaire (MVD) avec une AUC de 0,86. | Points clés : Corrélation radio-histologique coupe-à-coupe démontrant que Ktrans (DCE-MRI) et D* (IVIM) reflètent fidèlement l'expression de VEGF et la densité microvasculaire. ; Excellente performance diagnostique de Ktrans (AUC 0,861) et D* (AUC 0,860) pour identifier les contingents sarcomateux fortement angiogéniques. ; La séquence IVIM permet d'estimer la fraction de perfusion microvasculaire (D*, f) même chez les patients insuffisants rénaux ne pouvant recevoir de gadolinium. ; Optimise le rendement de la microbiopsie percutanée à Cochin en guidant le trocart vers le foyer tumoral biologiquement le plus actif. | Pratique Cochin : Directement applicable aux « bilans IRM 3T pré-biopsiques et au ciblage des zones hypervasculaires viables des sarcomes des parties molles » en RCP Sarcome à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] BACKGROUND: Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) expression and microvessel density (MVD) are established markers of tumor angiogenesis and key targets for anti-angiogenic therapy in soft-tissue sarcomas (STSs). Dynamic contrast-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (DCE-MRI) and intravoxel incoherent motion (IVIM) imaging are complementary magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) methods commonly used to assess angiogenesis in a variety of tumors. This study aimed to explore whether DCE-MRI and IVIM imaging could be used as non-invasive tools to evaluate tumor angiogenesis in STS based on MRI-pathology co-alignment. METHODS: In total, 70 patients with STS underwent 3.0-T DCE-MRI and IVIM imaging. The volume transfer constant (Ktrans), reflux rate constant (Kep), volume fraction of the extravascular extracellular space (Ve), true diffusion coefficient (D), pseudo-diffusion coefficient (D*), and perfusion fraction (f) were measured. Correlations between MRI parameters and VEGF expression or MVD were examined. Differences in MRI parameters between tumors with low and high VEGF expression were evaluated. Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves were generated, and interobserver reproducibility was assessed using the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC). An MRI-pathology control protocol was implemented to verify the spatial correspondence between MRI slices and their corresponding histologic sections. RESULTS: Ktrans, Kep, D*, and f were positively correlated with VEGF expression (r=0.673, 0.573, 0.692, and 0.386, respectively) and MVD (r=0.662, 0.595, 0.619, and 0.344, respectively). Ktrans and D* were identified as independent factors associated with VEGF expression and MVD. No significant correlations were observed between Ve or D and either angiogenesis marker. Tumors with high VEGF expression exhibited higher Ktrans, Kep, D*, and f values than those with low VEGF expression. The areas under the ROC curves for the four parameters in differentiating between tumors with low and high VEGF expression were 0.861, 0.844, 0.860, and 0.720, respectively. CONCLUSIONS: The study demonstrated associations between DCE-MRI- and IVIM-derived perfusion metrics and VEGF expression and MVD in STSs.
