AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte en risques compétitifs (*Bone & Joint Open*, juil. 2026, IF 2,5, Open-Access, 160 patients, suivi médian 3,4 ans) comparant la surveillance simple (n = 41), l'injection percutanée différée (n = 86) et l'injection percutanée immédiate (n = 33), ainsi que l'efficacité du polidocanol versus méthylprednisolone dans les kystes osseux essentiels (SBC/KOE) : l'intervention percutanée immédiate obtient une incidence cumulée de guérison significativement supérieure à la surveillance ou au traitement différé, et la sclérothérapie au polidocanol surpasse significativement les injections de méthylprednisolone tant sur le taux de consolidation osseuse que sur la prévention des échecs/fractures. | Points clés : Sur 160 kystes osseux essentiels, le traitement percutané immédiat offre un taux de consolidation supérieur et réduit la durée d'exposition au risque de fracture par rapport à l'attente vigilante. ; La sclérothérapie au polidocanol est significativement plus efficace que les injections intra-kystiques de méthylprednisolone (plus de consolidations, moins d'échecs). ; Les localisations du membre inférieur divisent par plus de 2,5 la probabilité de guérison spontanée (HR = 0,38), imposant un traitement interventionnel proactif. | Pratique Cochin : Éclaire directement la stratégie de « Sclérothérapie / Alcoolisation percutanée des kystes osseux essentiels (KOE) et choix du sclérosant (polidocanol vs corticoïdes) » en radiologie interventionnelle ostéo-articulaire. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Aims Simple bone cysts (SBCs) are among the most prevalent benign cystic bone lesions in children. The use of watchful waiting as management is debated, as are the type and timing of injection therapy. This study aims to compare clinical outcomes of watchful waiting and delayed and immediate percutaneous procedures in children with SBCs, together with the effectiveness of methylprednisolone and polidocanol. Methods All eligible paediatric patients with SBCs and minimal one year of follow-up were included from our tertiary musculoskeletal oncology referral centre (January 2000 to December 2024). Outcomes of interest were cumulative incidence of (partial) healing (CI-Healing), cumulative incidence of failure (CI-Failure), and prognostic factors for healing and failure. For CI-Healing, competing risk analysis was performed with failure as the competing event. For CI-Healing and CI-Failure of methylprednisolone and polidocanol, landmark analysis was performed. Prognostic factors were estimated using a multivariate cause-specific hazard model. Results In total, 160 paediatric patients with SBCs were included (mean age 8.7 years (SD 3.6); 128 (80%) males). Management consisted of watchful waiting (n = 41) and delayed (n = 86) or immediate (n = 33) percutaneous procedure. The median follow-up was 3.4 years (IQR 2.2 to 5.4). CI-Healing was significantly higher in immediate percutaneous procedures than in watchful waiting and delayed percutaneous procedures. Additionally, polidocanol performed significantly better than methylprednisolone regarding CI-Healing and CI-Failure. Lower limb localization was a significant negative prognostic factor for healing (cause-specific hazard ratio 0.38; 95% CI 0.18 to 0.82). Conclusion For treatment of SBCs, watchful waiting showed high chances of necessitating subsequent treatment, leading to lower CI-Healing and prolonged exposure to risk of failure. If treatment is required, polidocanol is a superior injectable compared with methylprednisolone, in terms of both healing and failure prevention.
