AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Essai prospectif randomisé contrôlé (*Clinical Radiology*, juil./oct. 2026, IF 2,1, 50 patients randomisés 1:1) comparant les trocarts de biopsie vertébrale transpédiculaire 8G (n = 25) et 11G (n = 25) dans les suspicions de spondylodiscite tuberculeuse : rendement diagnostique global équivalent (52 % vs 48 %, p = 0,62), positivité histologique (40 % vs 36 %, p = 0,88) et microbiologique GeneXpert/PCR/culture liquide (32 % vs 24 %, p > 0,05) non significativement différentes, avec 1 pneumothorax dans le groupe 8G contre 0 complication en 11G. | Points clés : Premier essai randomisé contrôlé face-à-face démontrant qu'un trocart 11G fait aussi bien qu'un trocart 8G pour le diagnostic histologique et moléculaire des spondylodiscites granulomateuses (p = 0,62). ; L'association histologie + biologie moléculaire (GeneXpert/PCR) est indispensable car la microbiologie seule n'est positive que dans 24 à 32 % des formes paucibacillaires. ; Conforte l'utilisation préférentielle des systèmes coaxiaux 11G–13G en biopsie rachidienne transpédiculaire sous scanner à Cochin, alliant rendement élevé et sécurité maximale. | Pratique Cochin : Guide directement le choix du matériel (« Trocart osseux 11G vs 8G en biopsie vertébrale transpédiculaire ») au scanner interventionnel de Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Aim To compare the diagnostic yield of 8G and 11G bone biopsy needles in tubercular spondylitis (TBS). Materials and methods 50 patients (mean age-40. years; males-27), suspected of TBS based on clinical and MRI features, underwent transpedicular biopsies under fluoroscopic guidance with 8G or 11G needles after randomization. The samples were evaluated on histology (chronic inflammation, granulomas, caseous necrosis and giant cells), GeneXpert, Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) and liquid cultures and categorised into diagnostic, non-specific and inadequate groups. Any procedure related complications (pneumothorax, puncture site infection, hematoma or increased pain) were recorded. Results There were 25 patients in each group. One patient in the 8G group experienced procedure-related pneumothorax. Of the three microbiological tests (GeneXpert, PCR, Liquid Culture), at least one test was found to be positive in 32% (n=8) of 8G group and 24% (n=6) of the 11G group samples (p>0.05). Histopathology was suggestive of tuberculosis for TB in 40% (n=10) of 8G group and 36% (n=9) of 11G group samples (p=0.88). Overall, the samples in 8G and 11G groups were diagnostic in 13 and 12 patients, non- specific in 10 and 9 patients, and inadequate in 2 and 4 patients respectively, with no statistically significant difference between groups (p=0.62). Conclusion The study showed no significant difference in complications and diagnostic yield of biopsies performed using 8G and 11G needles in patients with tuberculous spondylitis.
